<article class="env-example" id="040M">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/040M">Example <span data-tag="040M">39.5.2</span> <span class="named">(Roots of unity)</span>.</a>  Let $n \in \mathbf{N}$. Consider the functor which associates to any scheme $T$ the subgroup of $\Gamma (T, \mathcal{O}_ T^*)$ consisting of $n$th roots of unity. This is representable by the scheme </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mu _ n = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\mathbf{Z}[x]/(x^ n - 1)).  \]
</div>
  <p> The morphism giving the group structure is the morphism </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \begin{eqnarray*}  \mu _ n \times \mu _ n &  \to &  \mu _ n \\  \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}( \mathbf{Z}[x]/(x^ n - 1) \otimes _{\mathbf{Z}} \mathbf{Z}[x]/(x^ n - 1)) &  \to &  \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\mathbf{Z}[x]/(x^ n - 1)) \\  \mathbf{Z}[x]/(x^ n - 1) \otimes _{\mathbf{Z}} \mathbf{Z}[x]/(x^ n - 1) &  \leftarrow &  \mathbf{Z}[x]/(x^ n - 1) \\  x \otimes x &  \leftarrow &  x \end{eqnarray*}
</div>
  <p> Hence we see that $\mu _ n$ is a group scheme over $\mathbf{Z}$. For any scheme $S$ the base change $\mu _{n, S}$ is a group scheme over $S$ whose functor of points is </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  T/S \longmapsto \mu _{n, S}(T) = \mu _ n(T) = \{ f \in \Gamma (T, \mathcal{O}_ T^*) \mid f^ n = 1\}   \]
</div>
  <p> as before. </p>
